WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.570
Hello, and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.689
And today we're going to be
learning about orbital diagram

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:11.890
diagrams, which is going to further
illustrate how we can show electron

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.090
configuration in a more visual manner.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:18.700
The big reason why we need to
learn about this is because

00:00:18.709 --> 00:00:22.190
with electron configuration,
it can get a little confusing.

00:00:22.525 --> 00:00:26.514
Um, but there are three major rules
for how to actually be able to make

00:00:26.514 --> 00:00:30.605
sure you are filling everything in
correctly in order for us to go by.

00:00:30.974 --> 00:00:35.124
Um, the big reason is when you have a
periodic table and you're trying to read

00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:37.855
it to actually assign where electrons go.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.150
It's a little hard.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.860
So the first rule is the Aufbau
principle, which is we always fill

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.210
our lowest energy level first.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.769
Um, so we start at the bottom and we go
all the way up to the top, meaning we

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:56.760
start at the 1s orbital, move up to 2s,
then to 2p, and so on, filling as we go.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.430
Now, being that every orbital has so many,
um, or every essentially sub level has so

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.290
many orbitals available, One thing we've
got to keep in mind is we can only fit

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.090
two electrons in every single orbital.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.090
Okay, so when we are doing
those two electrons, why two?

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.780
Well, you've got to think this electron
is this little subatomic particle.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.180
The way to look at it is
think of it as like a ball.

00:01:20.209 --> 00:01:23.419
So if I have, let's say, a basketball
and I was trying to spin it on

00:01:23.419 --> 00:01:27.160
my finger, well, what direction
could you actually spin it?

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:31.560
Well, the one way to think of that
is right, it can either spin either

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.454
counterclockwise, or counterclockwise.

00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:37.705
Or you could try to, or start
clockwise or, um, counterclockwise.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.250
when you are trying to do that.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:46.550
So it's all about which direction
will this thing rotate on when

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.610
it's rotating around its axis.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.570
So this is the whole idea of spin.

00:01:50.919 --> 00:01:55.400
So when we are writing these in
orbitals, we'll show orbitals as

00:01:55.419 --> 00:02:00.840
a little line and we are going
to show the electrons as arrows.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:04.900
So one of them is going to be going
up, And the other one is going to

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:11.470
be going down to represent spin
up, as we call them, and spin down.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.310
So, now, this is an oversimplification
of the idea, but it's just enough for

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.100
us to be able to understand, um, to
understand this concept rather than

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.980
diving deep into the quantum mechanics
and understanding how these all work.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.930
Now, the last rule is Hund's rule, which
is essentially when you are writing your

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:38.130
electrons in these orbitals, some of the
orbitals are considered to be degenerate.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.419
Now, what is a degenerate one?

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.059
Well, an example of this would be,
let's say, if I had the p orbital.

00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:49.234
Uh, so P has three
individual orbitals to it.

00:02:49.575 --> 00:02:55.244
Um, it has one on the x axis, one on the
y axis, one on the z axis, if you were

00:02:55.244 --> 00:02:57.034
to try to think of this mathematically.

00:02:57.775 --> 00:03:02.480
So when we are trying to fill our
electrons in this, We fill one

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:09.140
electron in each individual orbital
first, or we start pairing them up.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.539
Now the reason behind this, while we're
pairing them up, is essentially, it

00:03:14.539 --> 00:03:20.600
comes to the analogy I love to use, is
let's say you are to try to Uh, stay

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.619
in a house with a bunch of roommates.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.810
Do all of you start kind of, kind
of jamming everybody into just

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.620
one bedroom inside the house?

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.950
Usually not generally you all want your
own private space, your own private room.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.400
Then you start doubling people
up when you run out of space.

00:03:37.745 --> 00:03:41.785
So, when it comes to like the p orbital,
right, you can only fit up to 6 electrons,

00:03:41.845 --> 00:03:45.795
but you're going to start putting
somebody in each bedroom first, before

00:03:45.795 --> 00:03:48.344
you start filling up everything, okay?

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.904
So, and then once you've run out of
room, you then go to the next floor

00:03:51.904 --> 00:03:53.795
and start filling that up as well.

00:04:03.255 --> 00:04:04.934
Starting off, let's start off strong.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.844
What does this entire picture look like?

00:04:08.344 --> 00:04:15.775
Well, uh, right here, what I have shown is
essentially a complete orbital diagram for

00:04:15.805 --> 00:04:20.445
an atom that could have, let's say it had
the maximum number of electrons possible.

00:04:21.025 --> 00:04:24.965
Every single one of these orbitals is all
the ones possible in your periodic table.

00:04:25.275 --> 00:04:28.765
So you can see on the far left
in yellow we have S, in purple we

00:04:28.775 --> 00:04:34.050
have P, and then in blue we have
D, and then F we have in green.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.374
Now, with these, it's a very common
question that you get asked on homeworks.

00:04:38.895 --> 00:04:44.175
Um, is in order to either draw these
or to try to list them in order.

00:04:44.655 --> 00:04:47.885
Now keep in mind we're always
going to have our little rules

00:04:47.914 --> 00:04:49.375
that are here with us, right?

00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:54.505
The first thing is how do you decide
where do electrons go in this entire mess?

00:04:54.895 --> 00:04:58.705
Well, remember, rule number one,
the Aufbau principle, electrons

00:04:58.705 --> 00:05:00.395
always start in their lowest level.

00:05:00.944 --> 00:05:04.504
So, one way to help you try
to remember how to start this

00:05:04.974 --> 00:05:07.395
is use the diagonal method.

00:05:07.924 --> 00:05:11.205
So, the diagonal method, this
is a chart that you can download

00:05:11.234 --> 00:05:14.284
anywhere on the internet if you look
up diagonal method for electrons.

00:05:14.665 --> 00:05:15.325
You'll find it.

00:05:15.325 --> 00:05:16.765
You can find it in any textbook.

00:05:17.245 --> 00:05:20.245
The way it works is it's written
in a very specific order.

00:05:20.305 --> 00:05:26.045
Notice how every single row
has the same quantum number.

00:05:26.495 --> 00:05:30.455
Now, keep in mind, you're doing
diagonal, meaning how we're

00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:34.925
going to show this, we'll start
in the corner, we go up, right?

00:05:34.925 --> 00:05:36.695
So one S two would be first.

00:05:37.175 --> 00:05:39.515
We then go to two s two.

00:05:39.515 --> 00:05:44.835
We do that one up and we
keep doing diagonals across.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:48.700
to show how all these are done in order.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.340
So we go from tails to heads.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:52.930
All right.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.120
So just to kind of illustrate
how does that work.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.350
So if you look at how I started it,
right, I went 1s2, then I went to here,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:09.429
right, so tails, up to 2s2, then I
went to 2p6, to 3p, 3s2, then to 3p6,

00:06:09.429 --> 00:06:12.999
then to 4s2, 3d10, and so on, right?

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:19.119
We can show how that order is as
we are representing this based on

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:21.130
what we are seeing here, right?

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:27.099
So we can write down the entire thing,
um, as far as we need to, to put that

00:06:27.109 --> 00:06:28.699
order based on the diagonal method.

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:29.729
And this can keep going.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.390
All the way through, through the entire
thing, um, to complete that picture.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:39.090
So, using the diagonal method is a
really, really easy way to remember this.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:44.520
Now, keep in mind, most professors and
teachers do not give you this on exams.

00:06:44.749 --> 00:06:47.550
So, it's something that you kind of
got to memorize how to put it together.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.050
Just keep in mind, right?

00:06:49.635 --> 00:06:54.945
Uh, that these are all going by
their individual, uh, rows of

00:06:54.945 --> 00:06:56.405
that periodic table, all right?

00:06:57.765 --> 00:06:59.974
When you are going through these,
we're going to be filling them in,

00:07:00.094 --> 00:07:03.564
and let's go through some actual
examples for how we can fill in those

00:07:03.564 --> 00:07:05.964
electrons using arrows to show it.

00:07:06.135 --> 00:07:07.885
The first example is nitrogen.

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:11.475
So nitrogen, the electron
configuration is 1s2.

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:14.015
2s2 2p3.

00:07:14.535 --> 00:07:16.525
So what does all this represent?

00:07:16.665 --> 00:07:20.694
Well, if you remember from my previous
videos on electron configuration, we

00:07:20.695 --> 00:07:24.065
learned that we could find these and
write them based on our periodic table.

00:07:24.345 --> 00:07:26.535
We're just learning how to
put them in orbital diagrams.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.650
First thing is all those exponents,
they're representing the total

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.530
number of electrons that are here.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.740
So we have seven total electrons.

00:07:35.319 --> 00:07:39.299
Now, the way that we're going to put them
in our table here is being that we have

00:07:39.299 --> 00:07:41.699
1s2, that's going to be our first orbital.

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:45.780
So we're going to put two
electrons in 1s2, right?

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.270
And notice how I did spin up.

00:07:47.755 --> 00:07:48.295
Spin down.

00:07:48.804 --> 00:07:52.794
So we first followed our Aufbau
principle by starting at the lowest

00:07:52.865 --> 00:07:56.994
level and then we did the Pauli
exclusion principle, which is all about

00:07:56.994 --> 00:07:58.604
them pointing in opposite directions.

00:07:59.165 --> 00:08:03.955
Now being that we just did that
one, what we do next is 2s2.

00:08:04.564 --> 00:08:10.825
So we go up, down, like so, and
then we will continue that trend.

00:08:11.275 --> 00:08:15.705
So being that now we have 2p3,
well, we don't just automatically

00:08:15.705 --> 00:08:16.965
write all six electrons.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.680
We now got to use Hund's rule, which
we have degenerate orbitals here.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:27.990
So we're going to assign each
electron gets their own room, okay?

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.660
So being that you have three
rooms available, why not put

00:08:32.709 --> 00:08:35.569
an electron in each one first?

00:08:35.869 --> 00:08:39.270
And when we do this, they all point
in the same direction, either all

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.270
pointing up or all pointing down.

00:08:41.699 --> 00:08:45.400
You choose, as the artist, they all
gotta be pointing in the same direction.

00:08:46.069 --> 00:08:50.730
And one thing you can check for yourself
is, do you have seven arrows, right?

00:08:51.109 --> 00:08:55.309
So you can kind of see here, I have
one, two, I have three, four, and

00:08:55.309 --> 00:08:58.069
then I have five, six, seven, right?

00:08:58.069 --> 00:09:00.614
So I have all of my arrows.

00:09:01.194 --> 00:09:01.555
here.

00:09:02.185 --> 00:09:06.275
Um, we can also use the count valence
electrons really easily as well.

00:09:06.795 --> 00:09:11.245
If you remember, valence is your
outermost shell, so everything that is

00:09:11.295 --> 00:09:17.165
in the two group here, which is your
highest quantum number, is your valence.

00:09:17.175 --> 00:09:22.385
So that means in this case we have
Five valence electrons and two

00:09:22.444 --> 00:09:24.745
core electrons for this example.

00:09:25.415 --> 00:09:26.675
Let's go on to the next.

00:09:26.885 --> 00:09:31.544
Sodium is 1s2, 2s2, 2p6, 3s1.

00:09:32.815 --> 00:09:35.491
So now what we are going to be
doing is filling in our, filling

00:09:35.491 --> 00:09:37.285
in our orbitals once again.

00:09:37.765 --> 00:09:40.245
So we first start with 1s2.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.380
We are going to put our two
electrons into that orbital.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:45.990
They're now accounted for.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:51.510
We'll go on to two S two, which is
now our next lowest energy level.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.800
We will fill in both of those.

00:09:54.569 --> 00:09:57.630
We'll move on to two P
six, then to three s.

00:09:58.685 --> 00:10:02.985
And as a result, remember, we're
filling one electron in each orbital

00:10:03.215 --> 00:10:04.585
first because of Hund's rule.

00:10:05.285 --> 00:10:09.644
Now we're going to come back and
fill in the remainder because

00:10:09.644 --> 00:10:14.555
we have, as you can see, we have
six electrons in that orbital.

00:10:14.944 --> 00:10:18.055
So we're going to put all
six in there as we go.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:22.689
Then for 3s, we only have one
single electron, being that's the

00:10:22.689 --> 00:10:28.710
lowest possible available, we will
put one electron in that level.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:29.670
It's like so.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.939
Now this is a orbital
diagram for sodium, okay?

00:10:34.379 --> 00:10:37.289
We can also still do the little
math too for ourselves, how many

00:10:37.289 --> 00:10:38.530
valence electrons do we have?

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.890
So once again, you're going to
look, what is your highest level?

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.970
So we have three here,
that is your highest one.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:51.165
So we have one valence electron, across,
um, all the other electrons here.

00:10:51.165 --> 00:10:56.194
So you can see we have a total
of 11 total electrons, but one of

00:10:56.194 --> 00:10:58.055
them is in your outermost level.

00:10:58.074 --> 00:11:00.564
So phosphorus, we're going
to do the same general idea.

00:11:00.824 --> 00:11:02.245
We're going to start from the bottom.

00:11:02.595 --> 00:11:06.784
So we start at 1s, and we're going
to put our first two electrons in.

00:11:06.915 --> 00:11:08.525
And notice how it's a pattern, right?

00:11:08.525 --> 00:11:10.584
We're going in the exact
same order every time.

00:11:11.005 --> 00:11:13.124
So 1s2, 2s2.

00:11:13.605 --> 00:11:15.705
So we have both of those completed.

00:11:16.325 --> 00:11:19.935
Now we're going to go to 2P6 and the 3S2.

00:11:20.605 --> 00:11:23.825
So we're following Aufbau principle
by doing the lowest first.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:28.400
And we're also doing Paul Exclusion
Principle by doing the opposite spins.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.630
Notice how we're doing Hund's Rule by
doing, filling in the whole orbital first.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:34.920
Then we do our s.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:41.429
Okay, now, so 3p, so
notice how we have 3p3.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.000
So how should we fill
this in with Hund's Rule?

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:47.230
Well, keep in mind being that
these are all degenerate orbitals

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:49.580
when we have our p orbital.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.045
So we're going to put
essentially one individual in

00:11:54.135 --> 00:11:58.415
each room first for phosphorus.

00:11:58.755 --> 00:12:01.415
And this is how we do an
orbital diagram for phosphorus.

00:12:01.775 --> 00:12:06.985
We go through and we fill, fill in
one at a time until, you know, until

00:12:07.074 --> 00:12:08.385
we don't have any electrons left.

00:12:08.644 --> 00:12:10.625
And we can even go through
and count them as well, right?

00:12:10.625 --> 00:12:13.814
You can see there's a total of 15
electrons for this, for this atom.

00:12:14.315 --> 00:12:15.445
So 15 total.

00:12:16.485 --> 00:12:20.235
And we can see that we have five
valence electrons available.

00:12:20.855 --> 00:12:22.755
Let's go on to our next example.

00:12:22.885 --> 00:12:27.715
Okay, so sometimes a more realistic
example that you might have to come across

00:12:27.915 --> 00:12:32.065
is writing the orbitals from scratch
where they're not there for you, which

00:12:32.095 --> 00:12:34.335
can prove to be a challenging problem.

00:12:34.795 --> 00:12:39.544
But we have the diagonal method
here, so how exactly can you do this?

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:46.300
Now, depending on your, your examples
that you have, you don't always have to

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:51.709
write this in order of, um, going from
bottom to top showing the energy levels.

00:12:52.079 --> 00:12:56.889
Sometimes you can also write them in,
in like from left to right horizontal

00:12:56.889 --> 00:13:02.939
as well, as long as you're labeling and
showing how does the energy increase.

00:13:03.574 --> 00:13:07.625
So in this case, an example, if I
want to go from left to right, we're

00:13:07.625 --> 00:13:11.935
gonna do the same thing as before We
start at the one s and we are going

00:13:11.935 --> 00:13:15.084
to write those two electrons in one s.

00:13:15.865 --> 00:13:20.005
Next we have two s, so we'll
write our one orbital for two s.

00:13:20.615 --> 00:13:21.574
What are arrows?

00:13:22.285 --> 00:13:24.655
We'll start go to two P and then three x.

00:13:25.134 --> 00:13:31.045
Now at two P, we have three orbitals
here, one in each going across.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:38.530
And then 3p5, we will fill 3 across
and then come back with 2 electrons.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:44.230
Now, looking closely at this one, what
is, what are some things to notice

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:46.540
in terms of counting the electrons?

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.910
Another thing to talk about is
paired and unpaired electrons.

00:13:51.509 --> 00:13:55.499
So, one thing you can see is that
there are many paired electrons here,

00:13:55.499 --> 00:14:01.420
so anything that's paired is when you
have two electrons going up and down.

00:14:02.805 --> 00:14:08.244
Unpaired is when you have a lonely
electron that's by itself in a room.

00:14:08.624 --> 00:14:12.234
So for this particular example,
we have one unpaired electron.

00:14:12.694 --> 00:14:14.494
Okay, so there's many different ways.

00:14:14.525 --> 00:14:19.140
Now, one of the things when you have an
unpaired electron, It generally means that

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:23.350
that atom will have a tendency to either
want to lose electrons to get rid of it,

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:26.550
or to gain electrons to get rid of it.

00:14:26.849 --> 00:14:28.559
They don't like having lonely electrons.

00:14:28.559 --> 00:14:29.709
They all want to have a partner.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:34.709
So for this one, I want to
look at chlorine minus, right?

00:14:34.709 --> 00:14:38.400
So chlorine as an anion, or
if you remember, anions are

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:42.780
negative, meaning that we are
going to be gaining an electron.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:44.880
So how would that happen?

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:49.170
What's going to occur is if you have
a cation or anion you're going to

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.380
edit your electron configuration.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:56.500
So we notice here how it
changed from 3p5 to 3p6.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.560
This shows the gain of one electron.

00:15:00.285 --> 00:15:03.455
So that's also going to change
in the electron configuration,

00:15:04.064 --> 00:15:07.944
and it's going to change over
here in the orbital diagram, too.

00:15:08.475 --> 00:15:14.255
So we'll add an extra arrow to represent,
oh, we now have chlorine, and it

00:15:14.255 --> 00:15:16.485
gets rid of that unpaired electron.

00:15:17.135 --> 00:15:18.035
Making it paired.

00:15:18.405 --> 00:15:21.295
Okay, so that's something
else that can be done as well.

00:15:21.295 --> 00:15:25.525
So a couple little tricks here that
are possible to have And we're still

00:15:25.525 --> 00:15:29.654
following all our rules as we go through
